      "source": [
        {
          "question": "e-5967",
          "field": null,
          "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
          "value": "Recall that the level surfaces for the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) = c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c = 0.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ {x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}-z & =0 \\\\ z & ={x}^{2}+4{y}^{2} \\\\ z & ={x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{1/4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level surface for $c=0$ is the elliptic paraboloid $z = x^2+\\dfrac{y^2}{{1/4}}.$",
          "category": "ma_capture",
          "file": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042953/example-5967.json",
          "file_sha256": "34407b19d9156e772939f645d85c74417fd14d95ed814ae0ebbcde233ef1fb0b"
        }
